@meta
id: 23-15
title: Join lines, with and without the space
chapter: 23
type: lesson
skills: format-gq
par: 20
minutes: 4
vscode: End, Delete, Delete, Delete
open: RELEASE-NOTES.md

@brief
The release notes were pasted from an email client that wrapped
everything hard. The changelog tool needs one line per bullet.

Three things to fix. The first bullet is split over three lines; make
it one, with single spaces where the breaks were. The second bullet is
split over two; same treatment. The dashboard link is broken across
two lines at `&to=` and `now`, and a space there would break the URL;
join those two with nothing in between.

@keys
| `J` | join the next line onto this one with one space |
| `3J` | join three lines into one |
| `gJ` | join with no space and no cleanup |

@hints
`J` on the first line of a bullet joins the next line onto it. It
removes the indentation of the joined line and puts exactly one space
in.
--
A count on `J` is the number of lines that end up joined, including
the one you are on. `3J` for the three-line bullet, `2J` — or just `J` —
for the two-line one.
--
`gJ` is the one for the URL: no space added, nothing removed.

@solution
    3G 3J  jJ  }j gJ  :wq<Enter>

Fourteen keystrokes. The file is short enough that `j` and `}` beat
searching for each line; par assumes you move, not search.

`J` is the opposite of `gq`: where `gq` breaks lines to fit a width,
`J` puts them back together. It is smarter than it looks. Leading
whitespace on the joined line is removed, one space is inserted, no
space is added before a closing bracket, and — with the `j` flag in
`formatoptions` — a comment leader on the joined line is dropped too.
The count includes the current line, so `3J` joins three lines and
`2J` is the same as `J`.

`gJ` does none of that. It takes the next line exactly as it is and
sticks it on the end of this one. That is what you want for a URL, a
long token, a base64 blob, or any string that a mail client or a
terminal has broken without your permission. If the joined line had
leading spaces, `gJ` keeps them, so check the result.

Both work on a visual selection, which is the easy way to join a
whole block you have already highlighted.

@start RELEASE-NOTES.md
# 2.4.0

- Refunds outside the thirty day window are now recorded with the reason
  REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED instead of failing. No money moves and the order
  still becomes CANCELLED.
- Declined payments raise PAYMENT_DECLINED with a 402 instead of a
  generic 500.
- Fixed the customer lookup in the seed script.

Dashboard: https://grafana.globex.example/d/payments?orgId=1&from=now-7d&to=
now

@expected RELEASE-NOTES.md
# 2.4.0

- Refunds outside the thirty day window are now recorded with the reason REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED instead of failing. No money moves and the order still becomes CANCELLED.
- Declined payments raise PAYMENT_DECLINED with a 402 instead of a generic 500.
- Fixed the customer lookup in the seed script.

Dashboard: https://grafana.globex.example/d/payments?orgId=1&from=now-7d&to=now
